WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.380
Hey, welcome back.

00:00:02.120 --> 00:00:05.560
Most alpha particles in Rutherford's experiment passed through the thin foil,

00:00:06.060 --> 00:00:08.260
but a small fraction scattered strongly.

00:00:09.200 --> 00:00:13.540
That supports a compact positive nucleus rather than positive charge spread

00:00:13.540 --> 00:00:14.240
throughout the atom.

00:00:15.240 --> 00:00:17.340
Let's calculate a special case carefully.

00:00:18.420 --> 00:00:21.700
An alpha particle approaches a heavy nucleus exactly head on.

00:00:22.780 --> 00:00:26.740
Treat the nucleus as fixed, neglect electron screening near the turning point,

00:00:26.740 --> 00:00:28.940
and use nonrelativistic motion.

00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:34.440
As the alpha approaches, repulsive electric potential energy increases while

00:00:34.440 --> 00:00:35.680
kinetic energy decreases.

00:00:36.740 --> 00:00:39.240
At closest approach, its radial speed is zero.

00:00:40.300 --> 00:00:43.000
Initial kinetic energy equals Coulomb potential energy there.

00:00:43.760 --> 00:00:49.000
The alpha charge is two e, and nuclear charge is Z e, so closest distance is k

00:00:49.000 --> 00:00:52.900
times two e times Z e, divided by the initial kinetic energy.

00:00:53.460 --> 00:00:58.186
Use gold with Z equal to seventy nine and alpha energy five megaelectron

00:00:58.186 --> 00:00:58.580
volts.

00:00:59.260 --> 00:01:03.370
A useful conversion is k e squared approximately one point four four

00:01:03.370 --> 00:01:05.540
megaelectron volt femtometres.

00:01:05.540 --> 00:01:09.500
The distance is two times seventy nine times one point four four divided by

00:01:09.500 --> 00:01:14.340
five femtometres, or forty five point five zero four femtometres.

00:01:14.340 --> 00:01:17.680
That is about four point five five times ten to the minus fourteen metres.

00:01:18.560 --> 00:01:22.880
This distance is much smaller than an atomic radius, but it is not itself the

00:01:22.880 --> 00:01:23.620
nuclear radius.

00:01:24.660 --> 00:01:28.460
For a non head on trajectory, the alpha retains tangential motion at closest

00:01:28.460 --> 00:01:32.220
approach, so setting all kinetic energy to zero would be wrong.

00:01:33.340 --> 00:01:35.980
Nuclear recoil also changes a more exact calculation.

00:01:36.600 --> 00:01:40.080
Our simple result uses a heavy fixed target and zero impact parameter.

00:01:40.920 --> 00:01:45.320
In JEE, ask what energy remains at the turning point before substituting.

00:01:46.200 --> 00:01:49.772
The experimental observation, the atomic model and the special closest

00:01:49.772 --> 00:01:50.180
approach

00:01:50.180 --> 00:01:53.400
calculation are related, but they are not interchangeable claims.

00:01:53.712 --> 00:01:57.352
Now let's use Bohr energy levels without losing the signs.

00:01:58.292 --> 00:02:03.452
For a one electron hydrogen like ion, energy is minus thirteen point six times

00:02:03.452 --> 00:02:07.992
Z squared divided by n squared electron volts in the elementary heavy nucleus

00:02:07.992 --> 00:02:08.312
model.

00:02:09.492 --> 00:02:12.892
Negative energy means a bound state relative to a free electron at rest

00:02:13.612 --> 00:02:14.192
infinitely far away.

00:02:15.132 --> 00:02:16.712
Consider singly ionized helium.

00:02:17.432 --> 00:02:21.292
Its nucleus has Z equal to two, but only one electron remains.

00:02:21.912 --> 00:02:24.952
The energy scale is fifty four point four electron volts.

00:02:26.252 --> 00:02:30.308
At n equal to three, energy is about negative six point zero four four

00:02:30.308 --> 00:02:30.772
electron

00:02:30.772 --> 00:02:30.932
volts.

00:02:31.932 --> 00:02:35.672
At n equal to two, it is negative thirteen point six electron volts.

00:02:36.652 --> 00:02:41.042
A downward transition from three to two emits the difference: seven point five

00:02:41.042 --> 00:02:42.892
five six electron volts approximately.

00:02:43.862 --> 00:02:47.932
Using h c equal to twelve hundred forty electron volt nanometres gives

00:02:47.932 --> 00:02:51.772
wavelength about one hundred sixty four point one nanometres, in the

00:02:51.772 --> 00:02:52.272
ultraviolet.

00:02:53.152 --> 00:02:56.972
The corresponding three to two hydrogen transition has one quarter of that

00:02:56.972 --> 00:02:59.592
photon energy, so roughly four times the wavelength.

00:03:00.352 --> 00:03:04.872
It is the familiar red Balmer line near six hundred fifty six nanometres in

00:03:04.872 --> 00:03:05.832
this approximate model.

00:03:06.592 --> 00:03:11.212
A spectral series is grouped by the final level; one line is only one

00:03:11.212 --> 00:03:12.472
transition within that series.

00:03:13.772 --> 00:03:18.372
For size, the Bohr radius formula is zero point zero five two nine times n

00:03:18.372 --> 00:03:20.932
squared divided by Z nanometres.

00:03:20.932 --> 00:03:26.342
The helium ion n equals two radius is zero point one zero five eight

00:03:26.342 --> 00:03:26.872
nanometres.

00:03:26.872 --> 00:03:31.492
Pause and compare the scaling: radius falls as one over Z at fixed n, while

00:03:31.492 --> 00:03:33.052
binding energy grows as Z squared.

00:03:33.772 --> 00:03:37.452
Do not use the same one electron formula for neutral helium with two

00:03:37.452 --> 00:03:38.512
interacting electrons.

00:03:39.512 --> 00:03:42.752
More accurate spectroscopy includes reduced mass and other corrections.

00:03:43.772 --> 00:03:48.152
JEE's elementary comparison needs the right charge, level numbers and the

00:03:48.152 --> 00:03:49.052
correct domain of the model.

00:03:49.512 --> 00:03:54.632
Finally, nuclear energy bookkeeping becomes much easier when every mass uses

00:03:54.632 --> 00:03:55.652
the same convention.

00:03:56.552 --> 00:04:00.852
For binding a nucleus, compare separated nucleons with the bound nucleus.

00:04:01.332 --> 00:04:06.032
If you use neutral atomic masses instead, pair hydrogen atom masses with

00:04:06.032 --> 00:04:08.652
neutron masses and the neutral atom's mass.

00:04:09.312 --> 00:04:13.892
That balances electron counts; small electronic binding corrections are

00:04:13.892 --> 00:04:16.172
normally neglected in our nuclear scale estimate.

00:04:16.852 --> 00:04:22.337
Let's use approximate atomic masses for fusion: deuterium two point zero one

00:04:22.337 --> 00:04:27.992
four one zero two u, tritium three point zero one six zero four nine u, helium

00:04:28.432 --> 00:04:33.647
four point zero zero two six zero three u, and a neutron one point zero zero

00:04:33.647 --> 00:04:35.672
eight six six five u.

00:04:35.672 --> 00:04:40.302
Initial mass for deuterium plus tritium is five point zero three zero one five

00:04:40.302 --> 00:04:40.972
one u.

00:04:40.972 --> 00:04:46.692
Final mass for helium plus neutron is five point zero one one two six eight u.

00:04:46.692 --> 00:04:50.052
Both sides have two atomic electrons in this bookkeeping convention.

00:04:51.152 --> 00:04:54.912
Mass decrease is zero point zero one eight eight eight three u.

00:04:54.912 --> 00:04:58.872
Multiply by nine hundred thirty one point five megaelectron volts per u to

00:04:58.872 --> 00:05:02.072
obtain about seventeen point five nine megaelectron volts released.

00:05:02.512 --> 00:05:07.712
The positive Q means a net rest energy decrease; energy appears mainly as

00:05:07.712 --> 00:05:09.892
product kinetic energy in this reaction.

00:05:10.832 --> 00:05:14.930
Do not confuse released energy with the activation barrier or with a claim

00:05:14.930 --> 00:05:15.152
that

00:05:15.152 --> 00:05:15.992
fusion is easy to start.

00:05:16.732 --> 00:05:20.712
For binding energy curves, divide total binding by nucleon number before

00:05:20.712 --> 00:05:21.832
comparing average binding.

00:05:22.712 --> 00:05:26.552
Moving toward higher binding per nucleon can release energy in suitable light

00:05:26.552 --> 00:05:31.352
nucleus fusion or heavy nucleus fission, but use actual conserved products for

00:05:31.352 --> 00:05:32.192
a numerical answer.

00:05:32.912 --> 00:05:37.052
This classroom uses approximate stated masses, not a precision nuclear data

00:05:37.052 --> 00:05:37.332
table.

00:05:38.172 --> 00:05:42.572
In JEE, check charge and nucleon conservation first, mass conventions second,

00:05:42.572 --> 00:05:42.816
then the sign of Q.
